# Manifest defaults must be realistic (R5)

The AIC PIM workspace's `app-config.toml` manifests carry two
defaults per key: `default_dev` (used by `cargo run` and the
dev-mode resolver) and `default_prod` (used by MSI / RPM /
notarized-pkg installs). The post-2026-05-19 win32 configurator
audit found multiple `default_dev` entries that were stale
placeholders (`server.example.com`, `12345678-1234-1234-1234-123456789012`,
`my-test-tenant`), so a fresh developer running through `cargo
build` → `Build-PimOfflineAgentMsi.ps1` → install → configurator
"Test connectivity" got a confusing failure on the first try
even though both halves of the dev stack were healthy. This rule
prevents that class of friction from recurring and pins the
contract for prod defaults that genuinely cannot be guessed.

## The rule

> **Every `default_dev` in any `app-config.toml` in this
> workspace must be a value that lets the reference dev smoke
> loop — `cargo build` → MSI install → configurator
> Test-connectivity — pass without operator edits. The
> reference dev stack is: `pim-offline-server` on
> `https://localhost:8443/`, the matching dev TLS cert from
> `core-assets/certs/localhost-dev/`, default Postgres on
> `localhost:5432` with the bootstrap credentials from
> `pim-installers/dev/postgres.env`. If a `default_dev` cannot
> satisfy that loop (a required secret, a per-tenant GUID, a
> field whose value depends on the operator's deployment),
> leave the value empty AND document the requirement in two
> places: (a) the `# operator must set before first run`
> comment in the manifest, and (b) the configurator's
> first-run banner that lists "fields you must set before
> Save".**

`default_prod` is unaffected — production defaults that require
operator input legitimately stay empty and surface in the
configurator's first-run banner. The rule's first half about the
dev smoke loop applies only to `default_dev`.

## Reference dev stack (pin these values, update when the stack moves)

| Component | Endpoint / value |
| --- | --- |
| Offline server (TLS) | `https://localhost:8443/` |
| Offline server (plain) | `http://localhost:8080/` (legacy / health) |
| Offline server TLS bundle | `core-assets/certs/localhost-dev/server.{crt,key}` (synced via `pim-installers/scripts/Sync-DevCerts.ps1`) |
| Offline server dev CA | `core-assets/certs/localhost-dev/dev-ca.crt` |
| Postgres host | `localhost` |
| Postgres port | `5432` |
| Postgres database | `pim_offline_dev` |
| Postgres user / password | from `pim-installers/dev/postgres.env` (development only — never committed) |
| Default tenant name | `Default Tenant` |
| Default tenant GUID | (empty — operator chooses; R6) |

These values are what the manifest `default_dev` columns must
agree with. When the stack moves (e.g. the server picks a
different default port), the manifests AND this table move
together.

## Required pattern: useful default_dev when possible

```toml
[[key]]
name = "SERVER_PUBLIC_URL"
value_type = "string"
default_dev  = "https://localhost:8443"
default_prod = ""   # operator MUST set before first run
description  = "Base URL the offline server advertises in installation JSON."

[[key]]
name = "DATABASE_HOST"
value_type = "string"
default_dev  = "localhost"
default_prod = ""
description  = "PostgreSQL hostname."

[[key]]
name = "DATABASE_PORT"
value_type = "uint"
default_dev  = 5432
default_prod = 5432
description  = "PostgreSQL port. Same default for dev and prod."

[[key]]
name = "TLS_PORT"
value_type = "uint"
default_dev  = 8443
default_prod = 8443
description  = "HTTPS listener port."
```

A developer who runs `cargo build` against this manifest and
installs the resulting MSI gets a daemon that, when paired with
a running `pim-offline-server` on the same loopback host, passes
"Test connectivity" on first click. Zero operator edits.

## Required pattern: empty default + first-run banner when not possible

Some values genuinely cannot be guessed. Tenant GUIDs, customer
secrets, per-deployment URLs — these stay empty in the manifest
and surface in the configurator's first-run banner.

```toml
[[key]]
name = "AGENT_UUID"
value_type = "string"
default_dev  = ""   # operator must set before first run
default_prod = ""   # operator must set before first run
description  = "Stable identity supplied by the operator at install."
operator_must_set = true   # surfaces in the first-run banner
```

```toml
[[key]]
name = "DEFAULT_TENANT_GUID"
value_type = "string"
default_dev  = ""   # operator must set before first run; do NOT use a magic GUID (R6)
default_prod = ""
description  = "GUID of the default tenant; operator selects at install."
operator_must_set = true
```

The configurator GUIs read the `operator_must_set = true` flag
and render a "Fields you must set before Save" banner on the
first run. The banner enumerates exactly the keys that fall into
this category, sorted by manifest order, with a click-to-focus
affordance per row. If the operator clicks Save with any
`operator_must_set` field still empty, Save fails with an inline
error message naming the field (no toast — the field stays
highlighted).

## Forbidden constructs

In every `app-config.toml` in this workspace:

- `default_dev = "server.example.com"` / any `.example` /
 `.test` / `.invalid` hostname. The dev stack does not run
 on those hostnames.
- `default_dev = "12345678-1234-1234-1234-123456789012"` or any
 placeholder GUID. This is also banned by
 [`placeholder-guids-are-empty.mdc`](placeholder-guids-are-empty.mdc)
 (R6); the empty string is the only sentinel.
- `default_dev = "my-test-tenant"` / `"changeme"` / `"do-not-ship"` /
 `"test-secret"` / any value containing the strings the runtime-
 status fingerprinter (R7) flags as a stale placeholder.
- `default_dev = "0.0.0.0"` for a connect target. (Bind targets
 are fine; connect targets must be reachable from the same
 host.)
- A `default_prod` that names `localhost` for a value an MSI
 install of an offline server will not have a localhost service
 for (e.g. an LDAP or KDC URL — these are deployment-specific).
- Asymmetric `default_dev` / `default_prod` where the dev value
 is realistic and the prod value is a copy of the dev value
 (this leaks dev endpoints into prod installs).

## Reference dev smoke loop (manual verification)

A developer or CI agent verifies this rule by running the loop
the rule describes, against the reference dev stack:

```powershell
# 1. Build the offline agent and the configurator from clean.
cd c:\analog-pim\pim-offline-client
cargo build --release

# 2. Build and install the MSI (per-product script under pim-installers).
cd c:\analog-pim\pim-installers
pwsh .\scripts\Build-PimOfflineAgentMsi.ps1 -Configuration Release
msiexec /i .\dist\AIC-OfflinePimAgent.msi /qb

# 3. Start the reference offline server on https://localhost:8443
#    (separate window).
cd c:\analog-pim\pim-offline-server
cargo run --release

# 4. Open the configurator and click "Test connectivity".
& "$env:ProgramFiles\AIC\OfflinePimAgent\pim-offline-client-configurator-win32.exe"
#    Settings -> Server Connection -> Test connectivity
```

Every substep (parse / DNS / TCP / TLS / app-level handshake)
must report success without the operator first editing any
field. If any step fails because of a placeholder default, the
manifest fails this rule and must be corrected.

## Worked violation → fix

**Violation** (today, conceptual, in `pim-offline-client/app-config.toml`):

```toml
[[key]]
name = "SERVER_PUBLIC_URL"
default_dev  = "https://server.example.com:8443"
default_prod = "https://server.example.com:8443"
```

The configurator's "Test connectivity" fails on a fresh install
because `server.example.com` does not resolve.

**Fix**:

```toml
[[key]]
name = "SERVER_PUBLIC_URL"
default_dev  = "https://localhost:8443"
default_prod = ""    # operator must set before first run
description  = "Base URL of the AIC Offline Server (set by the operator at install)."
operator_must_set = true
```

The dev smoke loop now passes on first click; the operator
installing on prod sees the field flagged in the first-run
banner.

## Where this is currently enforced

| Manifest | Status |
| --- | --- |
| `pim-offline-client/app-config.toml` | partial — Wave 1 Track C audits each `default_dev` against the smoke loop |
| `pim-offline-server/app-config.toml` | partial — same review pass |
| `pim-app-config` sample manifest | exempt (it's a sample, not a shipping product) |
| Future per-product manifests | this rule applies on creation |

